<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akropong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akropong is a town on the Akuapem ridge in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and the seat of the Okuapehene, paramount chief of the Akuapem state, since the 18th century. The Basel Mission settled there in 1835, and its seminary, founded in 1848, made the town a leading centre of education and of the Presbyterian Church in Ghana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akropong.jpg" alt="Akropong" width="104" height="101" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: West Akim Municipal District (AND) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akuapim_North_District_logo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Akropong lies north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, about an hour's journey away, and because of its altitude its climate is much cooler than that of the capital; a toll road climbs the hillside to it. The Akuapem hills were first settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guan people</a>, who came about 1300 from Attara Finam in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a> region. The town is known for its snails and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and for the Adowa dance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1730 the Hill Guan sought relief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akwamu">Akwamu</a> rule and asked the Akyem for help. After the Akwamu were defeated, the Akyem warriors and their families were given land at the present site of Akropong, and Amanprobi, the first settlement, was founded in 1730 under Nana Safori, who became the first Okuapehene in 1733. That year a covenant between the Guan and the Akyem, the Abotakyi Accord, established the Akyem royal dynasty at Akropong to give political and military leadership to the seventeen towns of the Akuapem hills, a dual state in which Akropong is the Akyem capital of a multi-ethnic traditional area. Its people follow matrilineal inheritance. The Basel Mission later ran a mission station in the town.</p><p>In 2002 and 2003 a clash between Akropong and neighbouring Abiriw cost many lives, and the divisional chiefs of Aburi, Larteh and Adukrom stayed away from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odwira-festival">Odwira festival</a> until five divisional chiefs signed the Koforidua Peace Accord on 27 August 2013. After the death of Oseadeeyo Addo Dankwa III in 2015 rival factions installed rival paramount chiefs, until Oseadeeyo Kwasi Akuffo III took the stool on 3 May 2020.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Akropong is the capital of the Akuapim North District in the Eastern Region and had 13,785 people in 2013. It is home to the Presbyterian Training College, the first teacher training college in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, a Presbyterian university and the Akropong School for the Blind. Each September or October it holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odwira-festival">Odwira festival</a> of victory, thanksgiving and harvest, with the purification of the kingdom, the parade of new yams, the cleansing of the stools in the Ademi River and a grand durbar before the Okuapehene's palace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akropong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He became the first Okuapehene, with his capital at <b>Akropong</b>, and the new state joined Guan and Akan communities under one paramount chief.</blockquote><a href="/state/akuapem">Akuapem</a></li></ul></section>
